  "content": {
    "id": "p41-25j-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "A bag contains nine sticks of lengths 1 to 9. Three sticks are drawn at random. Find the probability they can form a triangle. (Hint: The sum of the two shorter lengths must exceed the longest.)",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{17}{42}$.\n\nCount selections satisfying the triangle inequality, grouping by the longest stick.",
      "worked": "Write the selected lengths as $a<b<c$. A triangle is possible exactly when $a+b>c$. For longest lengths $c=3,4,5,6,7,8,9$, the numbers of suitable pairs $(a,b)$ are respectively $0,1,2,4,6,9,12$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{\\text{triangles}}&=0+1+2+4+6+9+12\\\\N_{\\text{triangles}}&=34\\\\N_{\\text{selections}}&=\\binom{9}{3}\\\\N_{\\text{selections}}&=84\\\\\\Pr(\\text{triangle})&=\\frac{34}{84}\\\\\\Pr(\\text{triangle})&=\\frac{17}{42}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
